- How would you adapt to architectures lacking native 128-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a branchless, correct popcount in O(1) time, a common low-level optimization in performance-critical code (DBs, networking) at senior-level shops like Oracle and Twitter.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit counting
- Parallel bit manipulation with masks
- 32-bit mask progression and final accumulation

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- Compare with hardware POPCNT; what are portability trade-offs?
- How would you test exhaustively or with randomized tests?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit value to a single parity bit using only bitwise ops, no branching.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based parity reduction
- Cross word reduction
- Constant-time bit twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (uint8_t)(x & 1);
}
```

